analyses.3:
All 12 trials in this search scored 0 on dev and on training success, as did the 84 trials before them. So the agent never completes an exact build even on training designs. That points to slow learning or blocked learning, not poor generalisation.

These ideas have now failed as stacks:
- orthogonal init with a small-gain start;
- GroupNorm;
- a raw skip into the head;
- factorised logits;
- per-cell footprint heads;
- DONE that sees the placement logits;
- a learned remap;
- bilinear stems;
- attention;
- recipe extremes such as 1 epoch with 400k steps, a myopic horizon (gamma 0.9), 1,024 environments, and lr 3e-3.

With 12 trials left and no signal, this round tries new families that differ a lot from each other:
- cumulative sums over learned per-layer codes, so the net can learn "lowest missing layer" and landing layer;
- MLP-Mixer token mixing;
- a weight-tied recurrent conv with about 400k samples;
- a wide trunk at 1 epoch;
- one footprint head shared across all brick types;
- a stack of all of the above.

analyses.4:
All 18 trials in this search scored 0 on dev and 0 on training success, the same as 84 earlier trials. Not one exact build was made even during sampled training. So there is still no evidence for any idea. Trunks, heads, layout remaps, DONE wiring and recipes all failed equally, so I cannot rank them.

This is the last round and failure costs nothing, so the six candidates aim for breadth and variance, not refinement. The main new idea is a noisy-AND footprint head: it sums a learned log-sigmoid score per covered cell, so one wrong cell vetoes the placement, which matches the exact-build condition. It is stacked onto different trunks and recipes.

The other bets have never been tested:
- higher capacity with 1 epoch (r about 2.8);
- a multi-scale U-Net trunk;
- a GLU-gated trunk;
- a learned bias per action;
- clip 0.1 with lr 3e-3.

If all six score 0, the program should say that what limits success is probably the world or the budget, not architecture or recipe.
